{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' collab/1789927000000-reviewer-debruijn-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md; sed -n '1,380p' docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-20 17:41:24 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-21 00:41:24 +07 (Indochina Time)\n\n# Adversarial design review: the de Bruijn projection\n\n**The design is not ready for implementation dispatch.** I found two P1 soundness defects and seven P2 defects that change the canonical form. They must be fixed in the document before D0, because every one of them alters the bytes that get hashed. The architecture, the layering and the vocabulary coverage are sound. This was a docs-only review. I edited nothing and ran nothing.\n\n## P1\n\n**P1 | §3 Scope algorithm | Duplicate parameter names resolve to the wrong binder.**\n- The VM binds parameters with `(into {} (map vector params …))` (`engine.cljc:46-51`). For a duplicate name the rightmost parameter wins.\n- The design pushes parameters right-to-left, so the source-leftmost has depth 0. The resolver searches nearest first and \"returns the first matching binder\". For a duplicate name that is the leftmost.\n- At runtime `(fn [x x] x)` behaves like `(fn [a b] b)`. The design projects it as depth 0, which is `(fn [a b] a)`.\n  - Two alpha-equivalent programs project differently.\n  - Two non-equivalent programs project identically.\n- Correction: state that, within one frame, a duplicated name resolves to the rightmost parameter, matching `bind-params`. Alternatively, make duplicate parameters a terminal diagnostic, but only if Yang already rejects them. I found no such check in the source.\n- Add `(fn [x x] x)` to the §8 test matrix.\n\n**P1 | §2 memo, §4 ordinals, §5 fingerprint | The fingerprint depends on node sharing, which is emission history.**\n- The emitter shares a node when it carries a pre-assigned `:eid` (`vm.cljc:546-561`).\n- §2 keeps one projected reference per `[source-eid lexical-context]`, so the projected graph inherits that sharing. §5 then hashes `row-count` and first-visit ordinals.\n- One term emitted once as a tree and once as a graph with a shared subterm gives different row counts, different ordinals and different hashes.\n- That contradicts §1 rule 5 (\"not of … emission order, or host identity\") and fails review item 3.\n- Correction: make identity structural, as a Merkle hash. `hash(node) = H(dimension ‖ tag ‖ scalar slots ‖ child hashes in order)`, and the fingerprint is the root's hash.\n- What the Merkle hash changes:\n  - The hash becomes a function of the unfolded term alone.\n  - Ordinals, the `-16` base, `row-count` and `root-ordinal` drop out of the identity.\n  - Hash-consing subterms is the only real compression the design can offer (owner constraint 4b). Dropping names saves very little.\n  - It matches the d1 content-addressing floor in `datom.md`.\n  - The `[eid, context]` memo stays, as an optimisation that avoids exponential re-walks. It no longer affects the output.\n\n## P2\n\n**P2 | §1 rule 4, §3 | The projection's domain with respect to macros is not stated.**\n- The design keeps `:yin/macro?`, so it admits input that is not yet macro-expanded.\n- Renaming a binder preserves meaning only after expansion, or under hygienic macros.\n- Example: macro `m` expands to the variable `x`. Then `(fn [x] (m))` and `(fn [y] (m))` project identically but mean different things.\n- I did not verify where expansion sits relative to \"post-Yang, pre-storage\". The walker ignores `:macro?` (`ast_walker.cljc:21`), and expansion is upstream stream topology.\n- Correction, one of two:\n  - Declare the domain to be the fully expanded AST, and make an unexpanded macro call site a diagnostic.\n  - Or state the hygiene assumption as a limit of the equivalence.\n\n**P2 | §2, §5 | `:yin/tail?` is derived data, and it is inside the identity.**\n- The emitter writes it only when the node map carries `:tail?` (`vm.cljc:564`).\n- The same program has two fingerprints depending on whether a tail-annotation pass ran. That is again emission history.\n- Tail position can be computed from the structure.\n- Correction: exclude `:yin/tail?` from the rows and the hash. Derive it; do not persist it.\n\n**P2 | §2 \"locates the entity carrying `:yin/root true`\" | Root selection conflicts with the claim that input order does not matter.**\n- The schema comment says \"the last one in batch order wins\" (`vm.cljc:460-461`, citing `yin.vm.macro.md` §2.4).\n- Correction: require exactly one root per projected graph, and make several roots a diagnostic. Alternatively adopt last-wins and withdraw the claim that input order is ignored for the root.\n\n**P2 | §2 \"`t` and `m` are ignored\" | `m` carries the assert-or-retract operation.**\n- The emitter's `m` defaults to `datom/default-op`, which is assert.\n- Ignoring `m` turns a retraction into an assertion.\n- Correction: honour the operation, or require assert-only input with a diagnostic for anything else. Only then drop `t` and `m` from the identity.\n\n**P2 | §6 adapter \"accumulates … until `:dao.stream/end`\" | A compilation stream that carries more than one program never delimits a graph.**\n- The emitter guarantees that the root datom is the last datom of a batch. That datom is the natural frame marker.\n- Correction: frame on `:yin/root`. Keep `end` with a partial graph as the diagnostic.\n\n**P2 | §2 grammar, §1 rule 4 | The policy for unknown attributes is missing.**\n- Unknown node types are diagnostics. Unknown attributes are not mentioned.\n- `:yin/macro-name` is in the schema (`vm.cljc:465`). `ast->datoms-with-root` does not emit it, and the design does not name it.\n- A real stream may also interleave `:yin.code/*` datoms and the expander's event datoms.\n- Correction:\n  - An unknown `:yin/*` attribute on a walked node is a diagnostic, because silently dropping a meaning-bearing attribute corrupts the hash.\n  - Other attribute namespaces are ignored.\n  - `:yin/macro-name` is named explicitly, with its rule.\n\n**P2 | §5, D3 | Byte-identical output across hosts is not yet achievable for numbers, and NFC normalization is a risk.**\n- ClojureScript cannot tell `1` from `1.0`; the JVM can. The design fixes integer width, and gives no rule for floats, bigints, ratios or characters, or for which one applies on which host.\n- NFC normalization is inherited from `datom.md:88`. It makes string literals that differ only in normalization collide, although they behave differently at runtime.\n- Dart has no built-in NFC.\n- Correction:\n  - Add a numeric canonicalisation table to D0: integers as int64, non-integral numbers as IEEE-754 doubles with the NaN and ±0 rule declared.\n  - State how a JS number is classified.\n  - Make an out-of-domain literal a diagnostic.\n  - Record the NFC collision as an inherited limit.\n  - Settle the Dart NFC source before D3.\n\n**P2 | §2 \"memoized by `[source-eid lexical-context]`\" | \"Lexical context\" is undefined.**\n- The key must be the stack of frame parameter vectors.\n- An intermediate binder shifts outer depths even when the subterm never references it.\n- Two distinct binder entities with equal parameter vectors are the same context.\n\n## P3\n\n- **§4 (P3).**\n  - **Operand order.** `:yin.db/operands` must never be stored as cardinality-many refs. `datoms->tx-data` expands `:yin/operands` that way (`vm.cljc:514-520`) and loses the order. The named form survives this, but the projected form cannot. Under the Merkle correction, references become ordered child hashes and the problem goes away.\n  - **Attribute name.** `:yin.db/*` reads as \"database\" next to DaoDB's `:db/*`.\n- **§4 and D2 (P3).**\n  - **Temporary ids.** Every projection reusing temporary ids from `-16` is harmless under a transactor.\n  - **Deduplication.** That reuse makes deduplication across programs impossible, which is another reason for content addresses.\n\n## Completeness (item 4)\n\n- Every node type that `ast->datoms-with-root` emits has a row in the design's table. I compared the table against `vm.cljc:565-630` arm by arm.\n  - The attributes are correct.\n  - The child order is correct.\n  - `:vm/store-put`'s `:yin/value` is correctly treated as data and not as a node.\n- The only omission is the attribute `:yin/macro-name`, covered under P2.\n- The essay's `(fn [count] (+ count 1))` is preserved: `+` stays free and `count` is bound at depth 0.\n\n## Rulings on the six open questions\n\n1. **Parameter depth convention.**\n   - Source-leftmost as zero is acceptable.\n   - Encode the index as a pair `[frame-depth, position]`, not one flat depth.\n   - A reference to an outer variable is then independent of inner arities. Under Merkle hashing, identical bodies therefore share across lambdas of different arity.\n   - The right-to-left push disappears.\n   - Position follows the duplicate-name rule from the first P1.\n2. **Shared-node duplication.**\n   - Yes, a node may be projected once per lexical context, as the design says.\n   - The converse must also hold: sharing must never be visible in the identity. That is the second P1.\n3. **Unordered literals.**\n   - Maps are encoded in canonical key order, using the canonical byte order of the encoded keys.\n   - Sets are encoded as their elements sorted by canonical byte encoding. Rejecting them is wrong: a set literal is a legal program value, and byte order is a total order available on every host.\n   - Reject only values that have no canonical codec.\n4. **Storage vocabulary.**\n   - Rename the namespace to `:yin.debruijn/*`. It matches the domain separator and avoids the \"db\" reading.\n   - Declare a published dimension descriptor, as `datom.md` lays out.\n   - A private segment type would put the hash outside the moduli space that the rest of the system verifies against.\n5. **Fingerprint algorithm and version.**\n   - SHA-256 is confirmed; `datom.md:92` already fixes it.\n   - Use the declared dimension's hash as the domain separator, following `hash(dimension-hash ‖ slots)` at `datom.md:66`. Do not use the ad hoc `\"yin.debruijn/v1\\0\"` string.\n   - A change of version is then a new dimension descriptor.\n6. **Source-to-projected index.**\n   - Keep it ephemeral by default.\n   - When persisted, it is a separate side index, never part of the identity.\n   - Under Merkle hashing it is a map from a source entity id to a node hash. That map is derivable, so persist it only where a diagnostics consumer exists.\n\n## Boundary (item 6)\n\n- **`dao.lease` and the waitset.**\n  - The design requires no interaction with either.\n  - §6 allows a waitset only if a composition owns several waiters.\n  - It introduces no lease fact, timer or callback.\n- **Runtime layers.**\n  - The AST schema, the walker, the semantic VM, `yin.vm.linearize` and `dao.stream` are all read-only inputs.\n  - The projection consumes the `:yin/*` datoms that the emitter already produces.\n- **Stream shape.**\n  - The adapter is an ordinary forward step.\n  - Framing on the root marker (P2 above) keeps it that way, because it removes the wait for end-of-stream.\n---\ndescription: Alpha-canonical de Bruijn projection of the named Universal AST\n---\n\n# DE BRUIJN PROJECTION OF THE UNIVERSAL AST\n\nStatus: design and implementation plan. This document defines a new\ncompilation-layer projection. It does not amend the Universal AST, its walker,\nor the semantic VM.\n\nThe compilation path is:\n\n```\nYang -> named Universal AST -> de Bruijn projection -> projected form\n                                  |\n                                  +-> canonical fingerprint\n```\n\nThe named form remains the bijective, stored and queryable representation. The\nprojected form is a derived value used for alpha-equivalence and deduplication.\nNeither form replaces the other.\n\n## 1. Architectural position and invariants\n\nThe projection is an interpreter over plain AST datoms. It is not a VM pass and\ndoes not execute literals, primitives, stream operations, continuations, or\neffects. It reads the named form and emits another plain-data stream.\n\nIt obeys the following rules.\n\n1. No AST, walker, semantic VM, `dao.stream` protocol, transport, lease, or\n   waitset change is required.\n2. All state is explicit in the step state: the input cursor, collected named\n   datoms, indexed graph, scope stack, occurrence memo, output cursor, and\n   pending output. No namespace atom, registry, callback, or clock is used.\n3. The stream adapter is a forward interpreter. A blocked input returns the\n   stream outcome unchanged; it does not busy-wait or hide execution in a\n   callback. The pure projection runs only after a complete root graph has been\n   collected.\n4. The projection is total over the emitter's declared node vocabulary. A\n   malformed root, dangling reference, cycle, duplicate structural fact, or\n   unknown node type is a terminal diagnostic, not an invented node.\n5. A projection is a function of the semantic named AST, not of temporary\n   entity allocation, datom transaction, metadata, emission order, or host\n   identity.\n\nThe projected form may be stored beside the named form in a content-addressed\nsegment. The named segment is the authority for rendering, queries, source\nattribution, and round-trip reconstruction.\n\n## 2. Input graph and deterministic walk\n\n`yin.vm/ast->datoms-with-root` emits d5 datoms with `:yin/*` attributes. The\nprojection accepts that same vector of datoms, or the equivalent stream of\ndatoms, and locates the entity carrying `:yin/root true`.\n\nThe indexed node grammar is exactly the emitter's grammar:\n\n| `:yin/type` | attributes read | child order |\n|---|---|---|\n| `:literal` | `:yin/value`, optional `:yin/tail?` | none |\n| `:variable` | `:yin/name`, optional `:yin/tail?` | none |\n| `:lambda` | `:yin/params`, `:yin/body`, optional `:yin/macro?`, `:yin/tail?` | body |\n| `:application` | `:yin/operator`, `:yin/operands`, `:yin/tail?` | operator, operands left-to-right |\n| `:dao.stream.apply/call` | `:yin/op`, `:yin/operands`, `:yin/tail?` | operands left-to-right |\n| `:if` | `:yin/test`, `:yin/consequent`, `:yin/alternate`, `:yin/tail?` | test, consequent, alternate |\n| `:vm/gensym` | `:yin/prefix`, optional `:yin/tail?` | none |\n| `:vm/store-get` | `:yin/key`, optional `:yin/tail?` | none |\n| `:vm/store-put` | `:yin/key`, `:yin/value`, optional `:yin/tail?` | none |\n| `:vm/current-continuation` | optional `:yin/tail?` | none |\n| `:vm/park` | optional `:yin/tail?` | none |\n| `:vm/resume` | `:yin/parked-id`, `:yin/val-node`, optional `:yin/tail?` | value |\n| `:stream/make` | `:yin/buffer`, optional `:yin/tail?` | none |\n| `:stream/put` | `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node`, optional `:yin/tail?` | target, value |\n| `:stream/cursor` | `:yin/source`, optional `:yin/tail?` | source |\n| `:stream/next` | `:yin/source`, optional `:yin/tail?` | source |\n| `:stream/close` | `:yin/source`, optional `:yin/tail?` | source |\n\nThe walk starts at the root and follows the table's child order. A vector\nattribute is ordered data; it is never converted to a set. A shared source\nentity is memoized by `[source-eid lexical-context]`, not by source eid alone.\nThis is necessary because the same named node can be reached under different\nbinders and therefore has different bound-variable answers. The same source\nnode reached under the same lexical context retains one projected reference.\nCycles are rejected: a Universal AST value graph is finite and acyclic even\nwhen an emitter-level eid is shared.\n\nFor a datom set whose semantic facts are equal, the root walk, child order,\ncontext keys, canonical node numbering, and emitted tuples are identical\nbyte-for-byte. Input vector order, tempids, `t`, and `m` are ignored except as\nnecessary to validate the facts.\n\n## 3. Scope algorithm\n\nThe pure core carries a scope stack. A stack frame is the ordered parameter\nvector of one lambda. The implementation pushes parameters right-to-left so\nthe source-leftmost parameter has de Bruijn depth `0`; the next parameter has\ndepth `1`, and so on. Entering a nested lambda pushes a new frame, so its\nparameters are nearer than all outer binders. Leaving the body pops that frame.\n\nThe resolver searches nearest frame first and then proceeds outward. It returns\n`{:bound depth}` for the first matching binder. If no frame contains the name,\nit returns `{:free name}` and preserves the original symbol exactly. Shadowing\ntherefore resolves to the nearest binder without consulting names from an\nouter frame.\n\nThe lambda's names are not emitted. Its `:params` attribute becomes\n`:yin.db/arity` with the integer count. A zero-arity lambda has arity zero and\nstill pushes an empty frame. `:yin/macro?`, `:yin/tail?`, primitive names,\nliteral values, keys, prefixes, buffers, parked ids, and stream operation keys\nremain semantic data and are copied after canonical validation.\n\nEvery child is projected under the scope at its occurrence. In particular,\nthe operator of an application and every operand are resolved in the same\nscope, while a lambda body is resolved after its frame is pushed.\n\n## 4. Projected form\n\nThe persisted projection uses ordinary d5 tuples with a distinct attribute\nnamespace. It is a derived segment, not a replacement for `:yin/*` datoms.\nFor a canonical projected entity `p`, the shapes are:\n\n* `[p :yin.db/type tag t m]` for every node;\n* `[p :yin.db/arity n t m]` for a lambda (never parameter names);\n* `[p :yin.db/bound depth t m]` for a bound variable;\n* `[p :yin.db/free name t m]` for a free variable;\n* `[p :yin.db/value value t m]`, `:yin.db/op`, `:yin.db/key`,\n  `:yin.db/prefix`, `:yin.db/buffer`, `:yin.db/parked-id`, and\n  `:yin.db/tail?` for the corresponding scalar attributes;\n* `:yin.db/body`, `:yin.db/operator`, `:yin.db/operands`, `:yin.db/test`,\n  `:yin.db/consequent`, `:yin.db/alternate`, `:yin.db/target`,\n  `:yin.db/val-node`, and `:yin.db/source` for the corresponding refs.\n\nThe reference attributes preserve the named graph's meaning and ordering, but\ntheir values are canonical projected entity ids. Source tempids are never\ncopied into the projected identity. Canonical ids are allocated in first-visit\nwalk order from the fixed negative base `-16`; allocation is part of the pure\nprojection and is therefore repeatable. A root marker is emitted as\n`[root :yin.db/root true t m]`.\n\nThe projection retains `:yin/macro?` as `:yin.db/macro?`; this bit affects\nmacro semantics and is not alpha-renamed. It does not retain `:yin/name` on\nvariables, `:yin/params` on lambdas, original source eids, or source datom\nmetadata. If provenance is wanted, it is a separate non-identity side index,\nnever part of the projected fingerprint.\n\nThe semantic projected record can equivalently be represented before d5\nemission as rows `[ordinal tag slots]`; d5 emission is the storage adapter.\nThe row form is useful for tests because it makes canonical numbering and\nfield order explicit.\n\n## 5. Fingerprint and canonical encoding\n\nThe alpha-invariant identity is the SHA-256 digest of the semantic projected\nrows, not of storage transaction metadata:\n\n```\nfingerprint = SHA-256( UTF-8(\"yin.debruijn/v1\\0\")\n                      || encode(root-ordinal)\n                      || encode(row-count)\n                      || concat(encode(row) in ordinal order) )\n```\n\nEach row is encoded as a tagged, length-delimited vector in this fixed order:\n`[ordinal, type, scalar-slots, ref-slots]`. The type tag and attribute names\nare namespaced keywords. Reference vectors retain their order. Maps, if a\nliteral contains one, are encoded by canonical key order; sets are rejected\nfrom the projected literal domain unless the existing named-form contract\ndeclares an ordering. Strings and symbols use UTF-8 NFC with a length prefix;\nkeywords and symbols include namespace and name separately; integers use fixed\nwidth little-endian encoding; booleans use `00`/`01`; nil has its own tag;\nbytes are length-prefixed. The encoding follows `docs/design/datom.md` and\nuses the same SHA-256 primitive on all hosts.\n\nThe fingerprint excludes d5 `t` and `m`, source entity ids, emission order,\nand any diagnostic/provenance fields. A literal value is encoded by the\ndeclared canonical value codec, including the repository's inline-or-hash\nrule for large compound values. Thus alpha-equivalent programs have identical\nrows, bytes, and hashes; non-equivalent programs differ unless the contract's\nsemantic value encoding itself identifies them.\n\n## 6. Stream composition and consumers\n\nThe implementation belongs in `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`, because it\nconsumes the `:yin/*` schema and must share no transport-specific code. It\nexposes two layers:\n\n* `project-datoms` — pure function from a complete named datom collection to\n  projected rows/datoms plus fingerprint;\n* a stream-to-stream `forward-step` adapter — drains one input stream using an\n  opaque cursor, accumulates plain datoms until `:dao.stream/end`, invokes the\n  pure core once, and emits the projected tuples on an output stream. It keeps\n  pending writes explicit and retries only under the host's normal cadence.\n\nYang and compilation orchestration use the adapter after Universal AST\nemission. Named-form storage, renderers, Datalog queries, source maps, and\ndiagnostics consume the `:yin/*` stream. Content-addressed deduplication,\ncache lookup, and alpha-equivalence comparisons consume the projected segment\nand its fingerprint. The semantic VM and `yin.vm.linearize` continue to\nconsume the named AST; the projection is not an execution lowering.\n\nThe adapter does not use a waitset merely because it is a stream interpreter.\nIf a composition owns multiple independent waiters, it may use the existing\nwaitset; otherwise it remains a normal forward step. No lease fact, lease\ntimer, waitset callback, or scheduler is introduced here.\n\n## 7. Implementation plan\n\n### D0 — contract and fixtures\n\nRecord this projection vocabulary, the parameter-order convention, the\ncanonical encoding version, and the source-node grammar. Add fixtures for the\nessay's `(fn [count] (+ count 1))` example, nested lambdas, shadowing, free\nvariables, all VM primitives, all stream primitives, shared nodes, and malformed\ngraphs. Completion means every emitted node type has a fixture and a stated\nprojected shape.\n\n### D1 — pure graph index and scope resolver\n\nImplement validation, root discovery, deterministic child indexing, cycle\ndetection, scope push/pop, and occurrence-context memoization. Completion\nmeans the pure resolver returns the same rows for shuffled input datoms and\nresolves nearest shadowing binders to depth zero.\n\n### D2 — projected rows and d5 emission\n\nImplement each table row above, canonical projected ids, ordered refs, and the\nderived d5 segment writer. Completion means projected rows contain no binder\nnames, no bound occurrence names, and no source tempids, while free names and\nall semantic scalar data survive.\n\n### D3 — canonical encoder and fingerprint\n\nImplement the versioned byte encoder and SHA-256 fingerprint. Completion means\nthe same projected rows produce identical bytes on CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD and\ndifferent alpha-inequivalent fixtures do not share a fingerprint.\n\n### D4 — stream adapter\n\nImplement the explicit forward-step state machine over `dao.stream`, including\nblocked, end, transport-error, invalid input, pending output, and terminal\ndiagnostic outcomes. Completion means no hidden collection, callback, or\ntransport access exists outside the adapter state and the focused stream tests\npass on all hosts.\n\n### D5 — pipeline wiring and storage integration\n\nWire Yang's post-emission compilation path to the adapter before projected\nsegment persistence. Keep named and projected segments addressable separately;\ndo not replace named storage or alter `yin.vm` consumers. Completion means an\nend-to-end Yang compilation stores both forms, render/query tests still read\nthe named form, and deduplication uses only the projected fingerprint.\n\n### D6 — end condition\n\nThe work is complete when all D0–D5 criteria pass, alpha-equivalent programs\nhave byte-identical projected rows and fingerprints, non-equivalent fixtures\nremain distinguishable, all emitter node types are covered, stream outcomes\nare handled without hidden execution, and the AST schema, walker, semantic VM,\nnamed storage, lease layer, and waitset have unchanged diffs.\n\n## 8. Test matrix\n\nThe focused suite must include:\n\n* renamed binders at one and multiple nesting levels -> identical rows and\n  fingerprints;\n* nearest-binder shadowing -> depth zero for the inner binder and the correct\n  positive depth for each outer occurrence;\n* free variables with different names -> preserved names and different\n  fingerprints;\n* changed literal, primitive key, arity, branch, stream operation, or\n  continuation marker -> different projected rows/fingerprint;\n* zero-, one-, and multi-parameter lambdas, including parameter order;\n* every `:vm/*`, `:stream/*`, `:if`, application, and\n  `:dao.stream.apply/call` emitter case;\n* shuffled datom input, repeated projection, and all three host encoders ->\n  byte-identical output;\n* shared nodes under equal and unequal lexical contexts;\n* dangling refs, duplicate facts, cycles, missing root, unknown type, and\n  unsupported unordered literal -> deterministic terminal diagnostics;\n* stream blocked, end, transport-error, invalid-answer, and pending-write\n  paths;\n* the essay's named example -> the expected arity/depth rows and a stable\n  fingerprint;\n* unchanged named `datoms->ast`, linearization, semantic execution, lease,\n  and waitset suites.\n\n## 9. What must not change\n\nThe following remain outside this design's authority:\n\n* the `:yin/*` AST schema, `ast->datoms-with-root`, `datoms->ast`, and the AST\n  walker;\n* `yin.vm` protocols and the semantic VM's environment, store, continuation,\n  and primitive behavior;\n* `yin.vm.linearize` and `:yin.code/*` executable datoms;\n* the bijective named form and its storage/query/rendering guarantees;\n* `dao.stream`, transports, `dao.lease`, and `dao.stream.waitset`.\n\nThe pressure points are explicit: projected segments require a new schema\nnamespace and a content-addressed segment type; large literal values must use\nthe existing canonical value store; and a future query API may need to expose\nthe source-to-projected side index. None of these pressures justify changing\nthe named AST or runtime layers.\n\n## 10. Open questions for the contract owner\n\n1. **Parameter index convention:** confirm that source-leftmost parameters are\n   depth zero (the plan pushes the parameter vector right-to-left). If the\n   owner prefers last-parameter-zero, change the convention before D1 and bump\n   the encoding version.\n2. **Shared-node semantics:** confirm that a node shared under two different\n   lexical contexts may be duplicated in the projection, as required to make\n   each occurrence's binding unambiguous.\n3. **Unordered literals:** decide whether maps/sets are part of the portable\n   literal domain. Maps have canonical key ordering; sets need a declared\n   canonical order or must be rejected.\n4. **Projected storage vocabulary:** approve the `:yin.db/*` attribute\n   namespace and whether projected d5 segments receive a published dimension\n   descriptor or remain a compilation-private segment type.\n5. **Fingerprint algorithm/version:** confirm SHA-256 and the\n   `yin.debruijn/v1` domain separator, or supply the repository-wide hash\n   primitive and versioning rule.\n6. **Source side index:** decide whether source-eid-to-projected-eid mapping\n   is persisted for diagnostics or remains an ephemeral compilation result.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"agent_message","text":"The review’s central correction is structural: canonical identity must be Merkle-based, while local row ordinals remain only a storage/layout detail. I’m applying that along with the binding, framing, macro, numeric, attribute, and dimension rulings."}}
